@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Character & String Data Types";
}

<section>

    <!-- =====================================================
         PAGE INTRODUCTION
         ===================================================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Character & String Data Types
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Server provides character and string data types to store
            text, names, addresses, descriptions, codes and other
            character-based information.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =====================================================
         WHAT ARE CHARACTER & STRING DATA TYPES?
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Character & String Data Types?
        </h3>

        <p>
            Character and string data types are used to store text values
            in SQL Server. These values can contain letters, numbers,
            spaces and special characters.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides different string data types depending on
            the maximum length of the text and whether Unicode characters
            are required.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         COMMON STRING DATA TYPES
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Character & String Data Types
        </h3>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Description</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>CHAR(n)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores fixed-length non-Unicode character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            CHAR(10)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>VARCHAR(n)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores variable-length non-Unicode character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            VARCHAR(100)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>VARCHAR(MAX)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores variable-length character data with
                            very large size.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            VARCHAR(MAX)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>NCHAR(n)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores fixed-length Unicode character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            NCHAR(10)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>NVARCHAR(n)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores variable-length Unicode character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            NVARCHAR(100)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>NVARCHAR(MAX)</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores large variable-length Unicode text.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            NVARCHAR(MAX)
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         CHAR
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            CHAR
        </h3>

        <p>
            The <strong>CHAR</strong> data type is used to store
            fixed-length character data.
        </p>

        <p>
            If the defined length is 10 characters, SQL Server allocates
            space for 10 characters even when the stored value is shorter.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Employees
(
    EmployeeCode CHAR(10),
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(100)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         VARCHAR
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            VARCHAR
        </h3>

        <p>
            The <strong>VARCHAR</strong> data type stores variable-length
            non-Unicode character data.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is commonly used for names, email addresses, cities,
            addresses and other normal text values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Email VARCHAR(150)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         NCHAR & NVARCHAR
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            NCHAR and NVARCHAR
        </h3>

        <p>
            <strong>NCHAR</strong> and <strong>NVARCHAR</strong> are used
            to store Unicode character data.
        </p>

        <p>
            They are useful when applications need to store text from
            multiple languages and character sets.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Customers
(
    CustomerId INT,
    CustomerName NVARCHAR(100),
    Address NVARCHAR(250)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         CHAR VS VARCHAR
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            CHAR vs VARCHAR
        </h3>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>CHAR</th>
                        <th>VARCHAR</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            Fixed-length data
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Variable-length data
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            Suitable when values have a fixed size
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Suitable when values have different lengths
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            Example: Country Code
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Example: Student Name
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         NVARCHAR EXAMPLE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example: Using String Data Types
        </h3>

        <p>
            The following example creates a Students table using
            different character and string data types.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Gender CHAR(1),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Address NVARCHAR(250)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            Here, different string data types are selected according to
            the type of text and storage requirement.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         INSERT EXAMPLE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert String Values
        </h3>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Gender,
    City,
    Address
)
VALUES
(
    1,
    'Rahul',
    'M',
    'Pune',
    N'Pune, Maharashtra'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         OUTPUT
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h3>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Output
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>StudentId    StudentName    Gender    City    Address
-------------------------------------------------------
1            Rahul          M         Pune    Pune, Maharashtra</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            The output shows how SQL Server stores character and string
            values in the respective columns.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         CHOOSING CORRECT STRING DATA TYPE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Choosing the Correct String Data Type
        </h3>

        <p>
            Select the string data type according to the length,
            structure and character requirements of the data.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>CHAR</strong> for fixed-length values.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>VARCHAR</strong> for variable-length
                non-Unicode text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>NCHAR</strong> for fixed-length Unicode text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>NVARCHAR</strong> for variable-length
                Unicode text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Use <strong>VARCHAR(MAX)</strong> or
                <strong>NVARCHAR(MAX)</strong> when very large text
                needs to be stored.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         SUMMARY
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned about character and string
                data types in SQL Server, including CHAR, VARCHAR,
                VARCHAR(MAX), NCHAR, NVARCHAR and NVARCHAR(MAX).
                We also learned the difference between fixed-length,
                variable-length and Unicode string data types.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


